      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 217.25,
      "end_seconds": 232.1,
      "duration_seconds": 14.85,
      "text": "Sorry, what was I saying, I've got an underactive thyroid. I'm hoping you can tell me something definite. I'm not great at remembering details, so bear with me."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0029",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 233.67,
      "end_seconds": 244.84,
      "duration_seconds": 11.17,
      "text": "taking everything into account, I don't have a a clear answer today: possible raised calcium from an overactive parathyroid is possible, and so is vitamin D excess"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0030",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 245.99,
      "end_seconds": 249.32,
      "duration_seconds": 3.33,
      "text": "what are the odds it's serious?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0031",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 251.13,
      "end_seconds": 287.72,
      "duration_seconds": 36.59,
      "text": "I can't promise yet, but most people with possible raised calcium from an overactive parathyroid do well once it's treated properly. I'd like to arrange a calcium and parathyroid hormone test for kidney stones. Let's get a kidney ultrasound, for kidney stones. I'd like to arrange a bone density scan for possible hyperparathyroidism. Drink plenty of water and avoid calcium supplements until the results. Come back or go to emergency straight away if the symptoms get worse."
    },
